| **Test Case**                         | **Description**                                                                    | **Notes**                                                                                                           |
| ------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **CSRF Token Missing**                | Tested behavior of application when CSRF token is removed from final request       | Application correctly rejects the request without a valid CSRF token.                                               |
| **CSRF Token Manipulation**           | Modified the CSRF token to invalid and replayed the request                        | The server correctly invalidated the session and responded with an error.                                           |
| **CSRF Token Reuse**                  | Replayed a previously valid CSRF token from a different session                    | Application rejected reused token as expected; tokens are session-specific and single-use.                          |
| **CSRF via Cross-Origin Request**     | Attempted to submit CSRF token using cross-origin HTML form and XHR requests       | Server implemented proper SameSite cookies and Origin header checks; requests were blocked.                         |
| **Token in GET / Cookie Leakage**     | Checked if CSRF token is present in URL, Referer, or in cookies                    | Token is passed securely via POST body only; no token leakage observed.                                             |
| **Reflected XSS – Registration Page** | Injected common payloads in input fields to check for reflected XSS                | All user input is properly encoded before reflection; no alert or payload execution observed.                       |
| **Reflected XSS – Streaming Page**    | Observed if user input reflects on the final streaming page                        | No reflected content found; the page appears to be rendered server-side with strict output encoding.                |
| **Stored XSS**                        | Attempted to store payloads in registration inputs and revisit the flow            | Inputs are sanitized/stored properly; no stored XSS vector observed.                                                |
| **DOM-Based XSS**                     | Analyzed JavaScript sources for dangerous sinks (innerHTML, eval, etc.)            | No vulnerable sinks tied to untrusted sources; DOM is secure on both pages.                                         |
| **HTML Injection**                    | Attempted HTML tag injection to manipulate DOM                                     | HTML tags were either escaped or filtered; DOM remained unaffected.                                                 |
| **Request Smuggling (TE/CL)**         | Ran smuggling probes with TE\:chunked and conflicting headers using Burp Suite     | Did not observe request desync or unintended behavior. Server handled Transfer-Encoding & Content-Length correctly. |
| **HTTP Response Splitting**           | Injected CRLF payloads in query parameters and headers                             | Application sanitized inputs; no header injection or splitting observed in responses.                               |
| **DNS Rebinding**                     | Verified application’s behavior with manipulated Host and X-Forwarded-Host headers | Host header was not modifiable. X-Forwarded headers were ignored or validated at reverse proxy.                     |
| **Cache Poisoning**                   | Tested for unkeyed input in cache headers and manipulated Host/X-Forwarded headers | Cache behavior is scoped correctly; headers are validated and input isolation was maintained.                       |
| **Open Redirect**                     | Injected redirect payloads in any `redirect` or `next` parameters                  | No redirection behavior observed; application uses hardcoded safe destinations or validation.                       |
